# History V3.4/V3.5 implementation guardrails

## Generated references

- Treat every `episodes/*-v3.4/` directory as a generated reference approval pack.
- Never implement behavior by directly patching those directories.
- Generated files may change only through the repository's generation pipeline.
- Behavioral changes belong in source code, planners, validators, typed schemas,
  CLI integration, or tests.

## Scope discipline

- Work only on the current numbered phase.
- Do not implement later phases early.
- Do not modify unrelated genres.
- Do not perform broad refactors unless a failing fixture proves they are required.
- Prefer the smallest safe change that satisfies the generated-artifact test.
- Analysis-only prompts (`prompts/history-v34-cursor/00-*.md`) forbid edits to
  production code, tests, schemas, configuration, and generated artifacts.
  Create only the required report and stop.

## Anti-loop verification

- Read `prompts/history-v35-cursor/00-restart-handoff.md` before resuming interrupted V3.5 work.
- Repository hooks enforce shell-command policy and edit-churn warnings; reload Cursor if hooks were added recently.
- Run exactly one `pnpm test:focused -- <test-file>` per shell command.
- Do not chain multiple acceptance tests, builds, or episode regenerations in one
  shell invocation.
- Do not use ad-hoc `node --input-type=module -e` debug loops; add or extend a
  focused unit/acceptance test instead.
- After two targeted fixes fail to move the same focused test, stop and report:
  exact command, test name, failure, classification, owning module, and the
  smallest follow-up.
- Do not regenerate the four-episode portfolio until every focused acceptance
  test for the active phase passes individually.

## Authority and research

- History authority remains `trusted-script`.
- Do not perform historical web research, source retrieval, or evidence assessment.
- Optional model use is limited to non-research semantic structuring.
- Do not mark trusted claims as independently supported.
- Record semantic-provider use separately from research-provider use.

## Deterministic authority

Application code owns:

- narration offsets;
- claim and entity IDs;
- entity and qualifier types;
- place resolution;
- coordinates;
- route geometry;
- diagram graph validity;
- timeline ordering;
- timing;
- diagnostics and approval gates;
- checksums and deterministic archives.

## Mandatory visuals

- Mandatory fixture maps and diagrams may not silently fall back to archival imagery.
- A failed mandatory visual remains an explicit blocker.
- Do not lower minimum map, diagram, pacing, or quality thresholds to pass tests.
- Do not replace semantic acceptance tests with snapshot-only assertions.

## Testing and completion

- Add failing generated-artifact acceptance tests before implementation.
- Acceptance tests must inspect real generated artifacts.
- Run focused fixture tests before portfolio regeneration.
- Regenerate all four episodes only in the generalization phase.
- Never claim completion without exact commands, exit status, output paths,
  artifact counts, SHA-256 hashes, and determinism evidence.

## Publishing

- Publishing work is separate from semantic planning.
- Do not mark production or publishing final without measured audio, rendered
  video, final thumbnails, reviewed captions, and human upload review.
